<article class="env-corollary" id="06RR">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06RR">Corollary <span data-tag="06RR">4.7.6.22</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories and let $n \geq -2$ be an integer. Assume that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a Kan complex. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ as a local $n$-truncation of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="058C" href="/tag/058C">4.7.6.13</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is a Kan complex and $F$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ as an $(n+1)$-truncation of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0557" href="/tag/0557">3.5.7.20</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume that condition $(1)$ is satisfied. By virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="058G" href="/tag/058G">4.7.6.19</a>, we may assume without loss of generality that $F$ is the canonical map from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to its $(n+2)$-coskeleton $\operatorname{cosk}_{n+2}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. In this case, assertion $(2)$ follows from Proposition <a data-tag="052N" href="/tag/052N">3.5.3.23</a> and Example <a data-tag="055B" href="/tag/055B">3.5.7.24</a>. The reverse implication follows by a similar argument. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
